(() => {
  'use strict';

  const ref = (title,url) => ({title,url});
  const source = {
    ct:ref('Kandathil & Chamarthy · Pulmonary vascular anatomy and anatomical variants','https://cdt.amegroups.org/article/view/19060/html'),
    vein:ref('Polaczek et al. · CT atlas of pulmonary venous variation','https://jtd.amegroups.org/article/view/36486/html'),
    plane:ref('Nakazawa et al. · Right upper-lobe venous planes on 3D CT','https://www.jtcvstechniques.org/article/S266625072030393X/pdf'),
    var:ref('Clinically important pulmonary vascular variations · Narrative review','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11170393/'),
    bronch:ref('NCBI Bookshelf · Anatomy of the tracheobronchial tree','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556044/'),
    lung:ref('NCBI Bookshelf · Anatomy of the lungs','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470197/'),
    larynx:ref('NCBI Bookshelf · Laryngeal muscles','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545265/'),
    trachea:ref('NCBI Bookshelf · Tracheal anatomy','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448070/'),
    phrenic:ref('NCBI Bookshelf · Anatomy of the phrenic nerves','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513325/'),
    fissure:ref('Cadaveric study · Variations in human pulmonary fissures and lobes','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6026823/'),
    mech:ref('Knudsen & Ochs · The micromechanics of lung alveoli','https://doi.org/10.1007/s00418-018-1747-9'),
    hist:ref('NCBI Bookshelf · Histology of alveolar cells','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557542/'),
    barrier:ref('Bioengineering the blood–gas barrier · Histologic and physiologic review','https://doi.org/10.1002/j.2040-4603.2020.tb00124.x'),
    surf:ref('Alveolar epithelium and pulmonary surfactant · Cellular physiology','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7152382/'),
    gas:ref('NCBI Bookshelf · Pulmonary ventilation and perfusion','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539907/'),
    hypoxia:ref('NCBI Bookshelf · Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499962/'),
    pleura:ref('OpenStax · Lung anatomy and pleura','https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/22-2-the-lungs')
  };
  const lung={}, alveoli={};
  function detail(target,id,course,physiology,relationship,variation,refs){
    target[id]={course,physiology,relationship,variation,sources:refs.split(' ').map(k=>source[k])};
  }
  const L=(...args)=>detail(lung,...args);
  const M=(...args)=>detail(alveoli,...args);

  L('lungs','Trace the hilar bronchus and pulmonary artery toward the center of a segment. Trace the returning venules outward to a larger collector, then medially to a pulmonary vein.','Alveolar ventilation supplies fresh gas while perfusion brings mixed venous blood. The local ratio of these two flows changes how closely end-capillary oxygen and carbon dioxide approach alveolar values.','The rib cage surrounds the costal surfaces. The mediastinum separates the two lungs, and the bases move with the diaphragm.','The model uses ten right segments and eight named left territories. Fused or separately named bronchi, accessory fissures and venous variants alter the map in individual lungs.','ct gas fissure');
  L('larynx','Follow the pharyngeal airway into the laryngeal inlet, through the space between the vocal folds and down through the cricoid ring to the trachea.','The posterior cricoarytenoid muscles abduct the vocal folds for breathing. Adduction helps close the inlet to the lower airway; vibrating approximated folds generate sound.','The thyroid cartilage shields the vocal folds anteriorly; the arytenoids move them posteriorly. The cricoid is the ring at the inferior end of the larynx.','The glottic aperture changes substantially between inspiration, phonation and swallowing. Only the external cartilaginous outline is shown in this thoracic view.','larynx trachea');
  L('trachea','From the lower border of the cricoid the trachea descends in front of the esophagus to its bifurcation. Its lower end moves with respiration.','Ciliated cells propel the mucus blanket toward the pharynx. An effective cough requires closure and reopening of the glottis together with expiratory muscle force.','Cartilage supports the anterior and lateral wall. Posterior trachealis muscle bridges the gap between the ends of the rings beside the esophagus.','Length, angle and bifurcation level vary with age, posture and the phase of breathing. The displayed rings illustrate their arrangement.','trachea bronch');
  L('tracheal-cartilage','Successive hyaline cartilage arcs run around the anterior and lateral tracheal wall. Annular ligaments connect adjacent arcs along the airway.','The cartilage resists collapse during pressure changes. The flexible membranous wall and trachealis allow the lumen to narrow during coughing.','The posterior gap in each C faces the esophagus. More distal bronchial cartilage breaks up into plates; bronchioles lack cartilage.','Ring counts and outlines differ. The cricoid immediately above the trachea is a complete ring, whereas the usual tracheal rings are open posteriorly.','trachea bronch');
  L('carina','The lower trachea separates into two openings divided by a projecting internal ridge. The carina lies near the sternal-angle plane in a resting adult.','Touch or inhaled particulate matter can stimulate vagal sensory pathways and trigger a cough that expels material from the larger airways.','The shorter right main bronchus angles more nearly in line with the trachea; the left tracks obliquely under the aortic arch.','The level and angle of bifurcation shift with respiration. A single point on a surface model indicates the ridge inside the lumen.','bronch trachea');
  L('right-main-bronchus','From the carina the right main bronchus descends to the right upper lobar takeoff. Beyond this origin the continuation is called the bronchus intermedius.','Its larger bore and more vertical course affect where aspirated material may pass. All inspired gas distributed here first enters conducting airways.','The right pulmonary artery reaches the hilum anterior to the main bronchus. The right upper lobar bronchus arises above the level of the artery.','A displaced or accessory upper-lobe bronchus can originate directly from the trachea. This scene shows the usual right main bronchial origin.','bronch ct');
  L('left-main-bronchus','The left bronchus descends obliquely from the carina into the left hilum, then divides into upper and lower lobar bronchi.','A change in its caliber alters resistance to ventilation throughout the left lung. Gas exchange takes place farther downstream after air reaches the alveoli.','The aortic arch passes above it. The left pulmonary artery curves above and behind the bronchus before distributing blood to the left lobes.','The left-sided lobar division is stable enough for orientation, while more distal upper-division and basal branch patterns vary.','bronch ct');
  L('bronchus-intermedius','This continuation of the right main bronchus descends from the upper lobar takeoff to the origins of the middle and lower lobar bronchi.','An obstruction here can impair ventilation to two lobes while leaving the right upper lobe ventilated.','The interlobar pulmonary artery initially lies in front of this airway; the middle lobar bronchus branches toward the anterior chest.','Its length depends on the positions of the upper, middle and lower lobar origins. It has no directly equivalent named left-sided segment.','bronch ct');
  L('lobar-bronchi','Each lobar branch begins at a main bronchus or at the right bronchus intermedius. Follow its intralobar branches to individual segmental bronchi.','An airway obstruction at this level affects ventilation across several segments. Its perfusion can persist, creating regional low ventilation relative to blood flow.','Lobar boundaries are marked externally by fissures; the lobar bronchus enters centrally from the hilum. The left superior branch supplies the lingula as well as the upper division.','Accessory or displaced bronchi can alter the usual five-branch plan without creating an additional anatomical lobe.','bronch ct gas');
  L('segmental-bronchi','Segmental bronchi enter the central part of their own territories and branch into subsegmental bronchi. The B number denotes airway destination, while A and S denote its arterial and parenchymal counterparts.','Smooth muscle and branch caliber determine how air is divided. Distal alveoli supplied by an open segmental bronchus still require perfusing capillaries for gas exchange.','Matching segmental arteries commonly follow these bronchi. Selected pulmonary veins mark the boundaries between neighboring segments, and other venous branches travel within a segment.','Left B1+2 and B7+8 designate combined apicoposterior and anteromedial basal territories here. Other schemes identify additional or individually arising bronchi.','ct bronch plane');
  L('bronchioles','Bronchi give way to noncartilaginous bronchioles, which branch into terminal bronchioles. Distal respiratory bronchioles introduce alveolar outpouchings before the ducts and sacs.','Smooth-muscle tone alters caliber. Multiple parallel small airways create a large total cross-sectional area, slowing bulk flow as gas approaches the acinus.','The small pulmonary arterial branch follows the airway; in the periphery pulmonary venules collect blood away from that bronchovascular axis.','The model samples distal branches. Airway generations and diameters differ among paths leading to different acini.','bronch ct gas');
  L('right-pulmonary-artery','The right branch of the pulmonary trunk passes behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava. Its anterior trunk and descending interlobar pathway distribute blood within the lung.','It conducts the right ventricle’s output to a low-pressure bed. Its branches narrow in poorly oxygenated regions, diverting some blood toward better ventilated alveoli.','At the hilum the right artery lies in front of the right main bronchus. The anterior arterial trunk commonly supplies A1 and A3; an ascending branch can reach A2.','The right superior lobe may receive separate or shared arterial branches. This model includes a separate ascending posterior branch.','ct var hypoxia');
  L('left-pulmonary-artery','From the bifurcation the vessel passes toward the left hilum, arches above the left main bronchus and curves posteriorly into the interlobar region.','Blood entering here still carries the oxygen content of systemic venous return. Its oxygen content rises downstream in the alveolar capillary bed.','The superior position of the left hilar artery differs from the anterior position on the right. The upper and lingular branches approach distinct portions of the superior lobe.','Origins of the left upper and lingular arteries are particularly variable. A mediastinal lingular origin is a recognized alternative to the interlobar pattern shown.','ct var gas');
  L('diaphragm','Muscular fibers from the sternum, lower rib cage and lumbar attachments converge on a central tendon. The two domes project into the thorax.','Phrenic motor input from C3–C5 contracts the muscle. Descent increases thoracic volume, lowers alveolar pressure relative to the atmosphere and draws air inward.','The pleural surface follows the undersurface of each lung base. The liver beneath the right dome contributes to its usual higher resting position.','Dome height varies with posture, abdominal pressure and respiratory volume. The still model shows one resting configuration.','phrenic pleura gas');
  L('visceral-pleura','This serous covering follows the external surface, turns inward along fissures and is continuous with the parietal covering at the root of the lung.','Pleural liquid reduces sliding friction; mechanical coupling across the fluid film lets chest-wall expansion pull the lung outward.','The visceral layer adheres to the lung. A potential space separates it from the parietal layer lining the thoracic wall, mediastinum and diaphragm.','In an incomplete fissure, the pleural cleft stops before the hilum and parenchyma bridges the adjacent lobes.','pleura fissure');
  L('cardiac-notch','The anterior border of the left superior lobe recesses beside the heart, then projects inferiorly as the lingula.','This indentation accommodates the heart within the thorax. Gas exchange still occurs in the surrounding lung parenchyma.','Find the notch on the anteromedial edge of the left superior lobe, superior to the lingula and anterior to the left oblique fissure.','Its outline reflects the relation of the inflated lung to the heart. It is a surface landmark rather than a separate segment.','lung pleura');
  L('lingula','This anterior tongue of the left superior lobe extends below the cardiac notch. The lingular division supplies a superior S4 and inferior S5 territory.','Both segments receive air and pulmonary arterial blood; their venous return commonly converges into a lingular trunk.','It corresponds in position and segment numbering to the right middle lobe but belongs to the left superior lobe.','V4 and V5 can drain individually or through a common trunk. An accessory left minor fissure may partially delineate the lingula.','ct vein fissure');
  L('right-oblique-fissure','Starting high posteriorly, the fissure runs downward and forward around the lateral surface toward the anterior basal margin. Its deep surface approaches the hilum.','The opposed pleural leaves allow relative motion of the inferior lobe against the superior and middle lobes.','The horizontal fissure meets the oblique fissure laterally; posteriorly the superior lobe abuts the inferior lobe directly.','Depth and completeness vary. Lung tissue may bridge part of this plane near the hilum.','fissure pleura');
  L('left-oblique-fissure','Trace this pleural fold from the posterior upper lung toward the anterior base. Its inferior side belongs to the left lower lobe.','The sliding pleural surfaces accommodate different movements of superior and inferior lobes during breathing.','The lingula lies above the anterior part of the fissure. Posteriorly, the inferior lobe spans a much larger area of chest wall.','Incomplete fissures leave bridges of parenchyma. Additional accessory fissures can delineate smaller territories.','fissure pleura');
  L('right-horizontal-fissure','The minor fissure extends from the anterior lung toward its intersection with the oblique fissure on the lateral surface.','Its pleural faces allow adjacent superior and middle lobe tissue to move with the changing thoracic volume.','The middle lobe lies below the horizontal fissure and above the oblique fissure on the anterior right lung.','This fissure may be incomplete or absent even when a distinct middle lobar bronchus identifies the middle lobe.','fissure bronch');

  for(const side of ['right','left']){
    const title=side[0].toUpperCase()+side.slice(1);
    const artCourse=side==='right'?'A right bronchial branch commonly travels from an intercostobronchial trunk to the posterior aspect of the right bronchus.':'Usually two left bronchial arteries arise from the descending thoracic aorta and approach the left bronchial tree.';
    L(side+'-bronchial-arteries',artCourse+' Distal branches follow the airway wall.','These systemic-pressure arteries deliver oxygen and nutrients to the conducting airways and supporting tissues. Some distal blood enters the pulmonary venous return.','Their vessels are much smaller than the pulmonary artery that carries blood to the alveolar bed. Rotate posteriorly to see the separate supply.','Origins can be orthotopic or ectopic, and the number of bronchial vessels varies. Only representative branches appear here.','ct lung');
    L(side+'-bronchial-veins',side==='right'?'Superficial right bronchial venous channels pass from the proximal bronchi toward the azygos vein.':'Superficial left bronchial venous channels travel toward the accessory hemiazygos or left superior intercostal venous pathways.','These channels return some of the blood used by large airway walls. Much of the deeper bronchial circulation drains through pulmonary veins, contributing a small venous admixture.','Trace this small systemic drainage separately from the much larger four pulmonary venous trunks.','Superficial systemic outlets and deeper connections can differ among individuals; the model depicts a proximal hilar segment.','lung ct');
    for(const level of ['superior','inferior']){
      const sup=level==='superior',src=sup?(side==='right'?'right upper-lobe anterior and central venous channels plus the middle lobar vein':'left upper-division and lingular venous channels'):(side+' common basal vein and the superior segmental vein V6');
      L(side+'-'+level+'-pulmonary-vein','Blood from the '+src+' converges at or near the hilum and enters the posterior wall of the left atrium.','This relatively low-pressure vein returns blood whose oxygen content depends on ventilation and perfusion in its tributary territories. Deep bronchial venous drainage also contributes.','In the '+title.toLowerCase()+' hilum, the '+(sup?'superior vein lies anteriorly near the upper-lobe bronchovascular structures.':'inferior vein lies low and posterior, near the pulmonary ligament.'),side==='left'?'The left superior and inferior veins can share a common trunk and atrial ostium. The model shows separate outlets.':sup?'The right middle lobe can have an independent atrial ostium or drain partly to an inferior vein.':'An accessory superior-segmental or middle-lobar tributary can alter right inferior venous drainage.','vein ct');
    }
  }
  const lobes={
    'right-upper':['right upper','right main bronchus','apical S1, posterior S2 and anterior S3','The upper lobar bronchus is eparterial: its origin stands above the right pulmonary artery.','Venous drainage may favor an anterior trunk, a central trunk or both.'],
    'right-middle':['right middle','bronchus intermedius','lateral S4 and medial S5','The lobe is anterior and lies between the minor and major fissures. The lower lobe accounts for most of the posterior lung base.','The middle lobar vein can drain independently or divide its return between superior and inferior veins.'],
    'right-lower':['right lower','bronchus intermedius','superior S6 and basal S7, S8, S9 and S10','B6 turns posteriorly before the basal bronchus descends. V6 and the basal common vein contribute to the inferior pulmonary vein.','Basal bronchial trunks and subsegmental branches may be shared, split or supplemented by an accessory segment.'],
    'left-upper':['left upper','left main bronchus','apicoposterior S1+2 and anterior S3 in its upper division, plus lingular S4 and S5','The lingula sits anterior to the oblique fissure. Upper-division and lingular tributaries commonly meet in the superior pulmonary vein.','Apical and posterior bronchial territories may be named jointly or separately, and the lingular vein can have more than one outlet.'],
    'left-lower':['left lower','left main bronchus','superior S6 and basal anteromedial S7+8, lateral S9 and posterior S10','Its superior segment projects posteriorly and cranially; the basal group lies over the left hemidiaphragm.','Some descriptions use S8 for the left anterior or anteromedial basal territory. A separate medial basal branch is variable.']
  };
  for(const [key,[name,origin,list,relationship,variation]] of Object.entries(lobes)){
    L(key+'-lobe','Follow the '+name+' lobar airway into '+list+'. Individual segmental arteries reach the central bronchovascular axes.','Air distributed to this lobe exchanges oxygen and carbon dioxide only where ventilated alveoli contact perfused capillaries. Venous tributaries collect blood at multiple levels of the lobe.',relationship,variation,'ct vein bronch');
    L(key+'-lobar-bronchus','The '+name+' lobar bronchus arises from the '+origin+' and supplies '+list+'.','This branch determines ventilation to the whole '+name+' lobe. Its subdivisions deliver airflow in parallel, each to its segmental territory.',relationship,variation,'bronch ct vein');
  }

  // Destination, anatomic neighbor and modelled arterial origin are explicit per segment.
  const segments=[
    ['right-upper-apical','B1','apical S1','ascends toward the apex of the right lung','S1 meets anterior S3 near V1b and posterior S2 near V2a','the right anterior arterial trunk','The anterior trunk can also include a posterior arterial branch.'],
    ['right-upper-posterior','B2','posterior S2','passes back toward the upper posterior chest','S2 lies behind anterior S3, with V2c in their boundary in the depicted arrangement','a separate ascending branch of the right interlobar artery','A2 can arise from the anterior trunk, an ascending branch, or both.'],
    ['right-upper-anterior','B3','anterior S3','runs forward into the ventral right superior lobe','S3 meets S1 along V1b and S2 along V2c. V3a lies within S3','the right anterior arterial trunk','The anterior and central upper-lobe veins collect subsegmental drainage in different combinations.'],
    ['right-middle-lateral','B4','lateral S4','runs forward and laterally in the right middle lobe','The medial S5 segment lies toward the heart; the horizontal fissure lies above both','the middle-lobe branch of the right interlobar artery','A4 and A5 can originate together or from separate middle-lobe branches.'],
    ['right-middle-medial','B5','medial S5','passes anteromedially toward the right heart border','S5 lies medial to lateral S4 and below the horizontal fissure','the middle-lobe branch of the right interlobar artery','V5 can join V4 before reaching the superior pulmonary vein, or drainage can be split.'],
    ['right-lower-superior','B6','superior S6','turns posteriorly near the upper end of the right lower lobar bronchus','S6 is above the right basal group but belongs to the inferior lobe. V6 often joins a basal venous root','the posterior aspect of the right interlobar artery','A6 may arise through more than one arterial branch. V6 can have an additional outlet.'],
    ['right-lower-medial-basal','B7','medial basal S7','descends medially toward the mediastinal portion of the right lung base','S7 lies near the medial base, neighboring anterior basal S8','the basal pulmonary arterial tree','A7 can have a shared stem with another basal artery. A distal label identifies its territory.'],
    ['right-lower-anterior-basal','B8','anterior basal S8','descends forward into the basal lower lobe','The right middle lobe lies anterior and superior across the oblique fissure; the two regions have different lobar bronchi','the right basal arterial tree','Subsegmental stems can share an origin with neighboring basal branches.'],
    ['right-lower-lateral-basal','B9','lateral basal S9','runs downward and laterally toward the costal base','S9 lies between the anterior and posterior basal territories near the lateral chest wall','the right basal arterial tree','A9 and A10 may share a common basal trunk before diverging.'],
    ['right-lower-posterior-basal','B10','posterior basal S10','descends posteriorly toward the costodiaphragmatic region','S6 lies more cranially and S9 more laterally; S10 abuts the posterior diaphragm','the right basal arterial tree','A9 and A10 can share a stem. The posterior venous channels have their own confluence pattern.'],
    ['left-upper-apicoposterior','B1+2','left apicoposterior S1+2','ascends and turns posteriorly in the upper division','Anterior S3 lies in front. Apicoposterior venous channels unite toward the superior pulmonary vein','the upper branches of the left pulmonary artery','The combined B1+2 label describes one bronchial pattern. Separate arterial branches may supply parts of this territory.'],
    ['left-upper-anterior','B3','left anterior S3','extends forward into the upper division','The lingula lies inferiorly, while S1+2 extends above and behind the anterior segment','the left upper-division arterial supply','A3 can share proximal supply with apicoposterior branches or arise separately.'],
    ['left-upper-superior-lingular','B4','superior lingular S4','passes toward the upper part of the anterior lingula','S4 is superior to S5 in the left superior lobe; the cardiac notch is near the medial border','the interlobar lingular arterial branch','A4 can instead receive a mediastinal lingular branch; arterial and bronchial divisions need not coincide.'],
    ['left-upper-inferior-lingular','B5','inferior lingular S5','descends toward the lower tip of the lingula','S4 lies above; the inferior lobe lies across the oblique fissure','the interlobar lingular arterial branch','The two lingular arteries or veins may have common or separate stems.'],
    ['left-lower-superior','B6','left superior S6','turns posteriorly near the origin of the left lower lobar bronchus','S6 sits above the left basal territories. Its venous return usually reaches the inferior vein alongside the basal root','the left interlobar pulmonary artery','The number of arteries reaching S6 varies and does not have to match the number of bronchi.'],
    ['left-lower-anteromedial-basal','B7+8','left anteromedial basal S7+8','descends toward the anterior and medial part of the left lung base','S9 lies more laterally and S10 more posteriorly. The left heart shapes the adjacent mediastinal margin','the left basal arterial tree','Some atlases use B8 and A8 for this territory; others distinguish B7 and B8 when a discrete medial basal bronchus exists.'],
    ['left-lower-lateral-basal','B9','left lateral basal S9','travels inferolaterally into the costal basal region','S9 lies lateral to the anteromedial basal territory and anterior to much of S10','the left basal arterial tree','A9 may branch together with A10, or a different initial basal division can be present.'],
    ['left-lower-posterior-basal','B10','left posterior basal S10','extends down and back toward the posterior costodiaphragmatic recess','The superior segment is above it. The lateral basal segment is on its costal side','the left basal arterial tree','The origins of A9 and A10 vary. Venous branches may drain adjacent parts of more than one basal segment.']
  ];
  for(const [id,b,name,path,near,origin,variant] of segments){
    const a=b.replace('B','A'),lobar=Object.keys(lobes).find(k=>id.startsWith(k)),lobe=lobes[lobar][0];
    L(id+'-bronchus',b+' '+path+'. It supplies the '+name+' territory of the '+lobe+' lobe.','Air passing through '+b+' continues through progressively smaller airways. If this bronchus narrows while capillary perfusion continues, its territory receives less fresh gas relative to blood flow.',near+'.','In this map the B label identifies the airway. '+(b.includes('+')?variant:'Origins shared with neighboring subsegmental branches do not change the named destination. '+variant),'bronch ct var gas');
    L(id+'-artery','From '+origin+', '+a+' '+path+' and follows the corresponding airway toward the '+name+' microvascular bed.','This arterial branch brings oxygen-poor blood to capillaries around air spaces served by '+b+'. Reduced flow with preserved ventilation increases local ventilation relative to perfusion.',near+'. The bronchus and artery occupy a segmental axis; collecting veins can follow different planes.',variant,'ct var gas');
  }

  const veinNotes={
    'right-upper-anterior-vein':['Descending on the anterior aspect of the upper lobar bronchus, this collector joins the right superior vein from its mediastinal side.','V1b from the S1/S3 boundary and the selected V3a channel from within S3 reach this anterior route.','Anterior-dominant, central-dominant and combined drainage patterns are described.'],
    'right-upper-central-vein':['This collector traverses the upper lobe between the posterior and anterior bronchi, then approaches the superior pulmonary vein from the interlobar side.','In this example, V2a and V2c enter from different segment boundaries. The central trunk is distinct from the posterior segmental artery.','The central vein can differ in caliber, course or presence among upper-lobe drainage patterns.'],
    'right-upper-v1b':['V1b follows the boundary between apical S1 and anterior S3 into the anterior venous collector.','B1 and B3 lie in their own segmental cores on opposite sides of this selected boundary.','The root receiving this boundary vein varies; the V1b label follows a published surgical naming scheme.'],
    'right-upper-v2a':['V2a tracks the plane between apical S1 and posterior S2 toward the central vein.','Its boundary path lies between B1 and B2; it can receive peripheral drainage from both sides.','Central and anterior venous pathways can receive different combinations of upper-lobe branches.'],
    'right-upper-v2c':['V2c travels between posterior S2 and anterior S3 and reaches the central collector in this pattern.','Its intersegmental position differs from the adjacent A2 and A3 arteries that supply the cores of their territories.','Its central termination is one documented pattern; the full upper-lobe map is variable.'],
    'right-upper-v3a':['This intrasegmental channel drains the anterior segment S3, running within its territory into the anterior venous collector.','Compare V3a within S3 with V1b and V2c on the boundaries of S3. A venous label alone does not identify a segmental plane.','The pattern of drainage and assignment of small subsegmental vein names depend on the classification used.'],
    'right-middle-vein':['Blood from the lateral and medial middle-lobe segments joins in a common vein that enters the right superior pulmonary vein.','The distal tributaries lie in the middle lobe, while its outlet joins drainage from a different lobe.','Middle-lobar tributaries may enter separately, have an independent atrial opening or drain partly or entirely toward the inferior vein.'],
    'right-middle-v4':['The lateral middle-lobe venous tributary travels medially from S4 to the common middle-lobe vein.','Its source lies lateral to the V5 territory, between the horizontal and oblique fissures.','V4 and V5 may merge or drain independently; their destinations can differ.'],
    'right-middle-v5':['The medial middle-lobe venous tributary joins the common trunk from the more medial S5 territory.','The S5 bronchus and artery bring air and blood into the territory; V5 gathers return from a different network of small branches.','A separate V5 outlet or split middle-lobe drainage may be present.'],
    'left-upper-division-vein':['The apicoposterior and anterior tributaries converge in the upper division and continue toward the superior hilar vein.','The lingular vein meets the upper-division return in front of the left bronchus in this arrangement.','Upper-division veins have described central, noncentral and semicentral configurations.'],
    'left-upper-v12':['Tributaries from apical and posterior parts of the upper division converge toward V1+2 and the superior lobar venous return.','The vein’s route relative to B3 distinguishes several upper-division venous patterns.','Separate subsegmental branches can run above or below B3 before reaching their common outlet.'],
    'left-upper-v3':['The anterior upper-division vein collects blood from the anterior portion of the left upper lobe and joins the apicoposterior venous return.','The lingular tributaries lie lower and can join a common superior vein separately.','Some small lingular or apicoposterior channels may instead converge with V3.'],
    'left-lingular-vein':['V4 and V5 merge within or near the lingula, then run medially to join the superior pulmonary vein.','It joins upper-division venous drainage near the anterior surface of the left main bronchus.','Independent superior and inferior lingular vein outlets and split drainage patterns are documented.'],
    'left-lingular-v4':['V4 gathers the return from the superior lingular region and joins the lingular venous trunk.','It lies cranial to inferior lingular V5. Both return blood from the anterior left superior lobe.','V4 can drain separately to the superior vein or contribute a subsegmental tributary to V3.'],
    'left-lingular-v5':['V5 drains the inferior lingular region toward the common lingular collector.','The lower tip of the lingula overlies the heart and remains within the upper lobe.','Separate lingular venous outlets and occasional drainage to the inferior pulmonary vein are described.']
  };
  for(const side of ['right','left']){
    veinNotes[side+'-lower-v6']=['Blood from the posteriorly placed superior segment S6 converges into V6 and joins the basal venous root to form the '+side+' inferior pulmonary vein.','The S6 tributary enters from above the basal group. Near the lower-lobe root the veins lie behind the lobar bronchus.','S6 can have accessory venous channels, occasionally with a separate outlet.'];
    veinNotes[side+'-common-basal-vein']=['Basal tributaries of the '+side+' inferior lobe join into a basal venous root. The root then meets V6 close to the hilum.','The basal root carries the collective return of several territories; V6 adds the superior-segment territory to the inferior pulmonary vein.','The number and order of basal confluences vary. Venous territories need not follow the arterial segment boundaries.'];
    for(const [n,name] of [[9,'lateral'],[10,'posterior']]){
      veinNotes[side+'-lower-v'+n]=['This representative tributary collects drainage from the '+name+' basal region and runs toward the '+side+' common basal root.','V'+n+' carries venous return from tissue near S'+n+'. Small tributaries can cross the segment boundaries defined by the bronchi and arteries.','Neighboring basal tributaries may share stems or divide their drainage. Its label indicates the principal territory in this map.'];
    }
  }
  veinNotes['right-lower-v7']=['V7 gathers medial basal blood and joins the basal venous confluence close to the mediastinal side of the right lower lobe.','The medial basal tributary approaches the pulmonary ligament side of the lower lobe. B7 and A7 enter the segmental core separately.','The basal venous root can receive different numbers of segmental tributaries.'];
  veinNotes['right-lower-v8']=['V8 drains the anterior basal region toward the basal venous root and then the right inferior pulmonary vein.','The anterior lower-lobe basal region lies below the oblique fissure; it is distinct from the overlying middle lobe.','The convergence of the anterior and neighboring basal tributaries varies.'];
  veinNotes['left-lower-v78']=['A representative venous channel from the anteromedial basal region enters the basal root on the left.','This territory is supplied by B7+8 in the illustrated scheme. A venous drainage territory can extend beyond that bronchial subdivision.','Left anteromedial vein labels vary with the segmentation scheme. The model does not assume two separate veins when the territories are combined.'];
  const veins=window.lungStructures.filter(s=>s.type==='Venous tributary');
  for(const v of veins){
    const [course,relationship,variation]=veinNotes[v.id];
    const destination=window.lungStructures.find(s=>s.id===v.related[0]).name.toLowerCase();
    const physiology=v.id.startsWith('right-upper-v')?'Multiple small venules feed this channel. Their combined blood continues into the '+destination+'; the venous route reveals which tissue shares the downstream collector.':'The blood in this tributary has passed through alveolar capillaries. Its next named collector is the '+destination+', carrying that regional return toward the left atrium.';
    L(v.id,course,physiology,relationship,variation,v.id.startsWith('right-upper-')?'plane vein ct':'vein var ct');
  }

  M('alveoli','An alveolus is a small air recess opening onto a respiratory bronchiole, duct or common atrium. In this longitudinal cutaway, trace the open duct to the recesses around it.','Oxygen moves from air to plasma and then binds hemoglobin. Carbon dioxide moves from blood toward the air when its partial pressure is higher in the arriving blood.','A partition between adjoining air spaces contains an interconnected capillary sheet. The shared septa mechanically couple neighboring alveoli.','Real shapes are irregular and change with lung volume. The visible septa, cells and capillaries are enlarged independently for inspection.','mech hist gas');
  M('terminal-bronchiole','The final conducting branch gives rise to respiratory bronchioles; along the terminal branch, its wall is continuous around the lumen.','Its epithelial lining and smooth muscle help maintain and regulate flow into the acinus. Air must travel farther before encountering alveolar exchange surfaces.','The first alveolar outpouchings mark the transition to the respiratory bronchiole. Cartilage and submucosal glands have disappeared by this level.','A terminal bronchiole can supply multiple respiratory bronchioles. A single path is opened here to show the transition.','bronch mech hist');
  M('respiratory-bronchiole','Initially, isolated alveolar openings interrupt the bronchiolar wall. More distal branches have progressively more openings before becoming alveolar ducts.','This transitional airway still conducts air while the alveolated portions of its wall add exchange surface. Gas movement becomes increasingly dependent on diffusion distally.','Continuous bronchiolar epithelium alternates with thin alveolar lining. In the cutaway, the first side pockets mark that transition.','The number of transitional generations and spacing of the first alveoli vary along different paths within an acinus.','mech hist bronch');
  M('alveolar-duct','Follow the long open passage framed by septal free edges. Alveolar openings occupy most of its boundary, and a blind atrium can be reached distally.','Gas distributes from the duct into its adjoining alveolar spaces largely by diffusion. The duct lumen itself is a passage through a continuous network of air spaces.','The septal tips contain supporting fibers around the mouths. Unlike a cartilage-supported conducting tube, the duct wall is largely a succession of alveolar entrances.','Many ducts branch repeatedly. The open face exposes one longitudinal path through the terminal region.','mech hist');
  M('alveolar-sac','An alveolar sac is the terminal common air space into which multiple alveoli open. Follow the modeled duct to its blind distal end.','The atrium distributes inspired gas among neighboring recesses. Gas crosses the thin air–blood barrier lining those recesses wherever capillary blood flows past.','The open shared atrium differs from each individual pocket surrounding it; the intervening tissue ridges belong to adjacent alveolar walls.','The boundaries of sacs and ducts can look different in a two-dimensional section through the same three-dimensional acinus.','mech hist');
  M('alveolar-septum','One partition extends from a free edge beside the duct into the peripheral parenchyma, separating air spaces on either side. Within it are capillaries and a supporting interstitium.','Tension in the septal fiber network and the surface film helps transmit forces among neighboring air spaces during inflation and deflation.','On a thin side of a capillary, epithelial and endothelial cells approach closely; a thicker portion can accommodate fibers, nuclei and interstitial cells.','Thickness varies within the same septum. The free rim and the full thickness of a septal wall are distinct anatomical features.','mech barrier hist');
  M('pulmonary-arteriole','A terminal pulmonary arterial branch divides upstream of the alveolar capillary network. In the cutaway the blue inlet reaches septal channels at several points.','Low alveolar oxygen can constrict nearby small pulmonary arteries and arterioles, changing local perfusion. Widespread hypoxia can raise overall pulmonary vascular resistance.','Its upstream connection is to the pulmonary arterial tree; blood leaving the capillary bed is collected by separate venules.','Arterioles and venules contact different parts of a converging network. There is no single inlet–outlet pair for every air pocket.','ct hypoxia barrier');
  M('alveolar-capillaries','Small inflow branches feed a mesh of communicating blood channels embedded in the septa. Multiple paths then converge toward draining venules.','The combination of surface area, diffusion distance, partial-pressure differences and blood transit determines gas transfer. Hemoglobin binding helps sustain oxygen uptake along the capillary.','The capillary endothelium is separated from alveolar air by epithelial and basement-membrane layers. The opened channel at the cut edge contains an erythrocyte.','Channels change shape and recruitment as vascular and alveolar pressures change. The diagram enlarges the mesh to show its connections.','barrier mech gas');
  M('pulmonary-venule','At the outflow side of the septal bed, small venous channels coalesce. They feed intrapulmonary venous tributaries and ultimately the left atrium.','Venular blood combines contributions from many capillaries. Its oxygen content depends on the ventilation–perfusion conditions of those contributing regions.','Venous collectors tend to occupy peripheral or interlobular connective-tissue planes at larger scales. Pulmonary arterial branches more often follow airways.','A venule drains a network shared by multiple alveoli. The cluster illustrates a representative collector rather than a separate vessel for each pocket.','ct barrier gas');
  M('respiratory-membrane','From alveolar air, a gas crosses the liquid surface film, an epithelial cell, basal laminae and a capillary endothelial cell before reaching plasma and the red cell.','Fick’s relationship predicts higher diffusive transfer with greater exchange area or a larger partial-pressure gradient. A longer diffusion path reduces transfer when other conditions are unchanged.','Inspect the enlarged bands beside the open capillary. Type I epithelium faces air; endothelium faces the blood. Their basal laminae closely appose on the thin side.','Basal laminae can separate around thicker supporting interstitial tissue. The model exaggerates layer thickness and color to make the order visible.','barrier mech hist');
  M('type-i-cell','A flattened type I pneumocyte extends broad, thin cytoplasmic sheets across the alveolar surface and joins neighboring epithelial cells.','Its attenuated cytoplasm shortens the path for diffusion; epithelial junctions help maintain the separation of air-space liquid and interstitial fluid.','The apical face meets the alveolar lining fluid. Its basal surface rests on a lamina that can fuse with the capillary endothelial basal lamina.','The thin processes and thicker nuclear portion belong to the same cell. The model highlights a broad process at the capillary cutaway.','hist surf barrier');
  M('type-ii-cell','Cuboidal type II cells occur among the thin type I cells, often near junctions of septa. Their lamellar bodies store surfactant material before secretion.','Surfactant lowers surface tension at the air–liquid interface. Type II cells also divide and can give rise to type I cells during epithelial repair.','These cells occupy relatively little exposed surface compared with the large thin sheets of type I cells. The selected cell sits near a septal junction.','Type II cell shape, abundance and proliferative state depend on development and epithelial injury. The feature shown is a representative cell.','surf hist mech');
  M('erythrocyte','The cell passes from an afferent pulmonary arteriole through narrow septal capillaries into a venule. The opened capillary shows a single enlarged cell.','Oxygen diffuses through plasma and the cell membrane, then binds hemoglobin. Carbon dioxide can leave the blood as dissolved gas is replenished from bicarbonate and other blood stores.','The cell remains within the blood space. Endothelium, basal laminae and alveolar epithelium separate its hemoglobin from alveolar air.','Its resting biconcave shape deforms during capillary transit. A single static cell cannot represent the full sequence of shapes along a capillary.','barrier gas');
  M('capillary-endothelium','A continuous cellular lining follows the septal capillary from its arterial to venous side. The exposed inner wall in the cutaway faces the blood space.','Flat endothelial extensions limit diffusion distance. Endothelial junctions and barrier properties help regulate fluid movement out of the capillary.','The endothelial basal surface faces the basal lamina; its luminal surface faces plasma and passing erythrocytes. Epithelial cells line the opposite, air-facing side.','The nucleus is thicker than the thin peripheral processes. Not every portion of the endothelial wall is equally close to an alveolar air space.','barrier hist');
  M('fused-basal-laminae','On the thin gas-exchanging side of a septal capillary, basal laminae beneath the epithelium and endothelium are joined at their interface.','Fusion provides support with minimal intervening tissue. Where material accumulates between air and capillary blood, the greater path length can reduce diffusion.','These extracellular layers lie between two cellular layers. They do not line the capillary lumen or directly contact the air–liquid interface.','Fusion is regional. Thicker septal areas contain more interstitial connective tissue between the two basal laminae.','barrier hist mech');
  M('surfactant-film','Type II cells secrete surfactant into the alveolar liquid lining. Its surface-active components spread at the boundary between that liquid and the gas.','Reducing surface tension lowers the pressure needed to expand the air spaces and helps stabilize them during deflation. The film changes organization during each breathing cycle.','The film lies on the air side of the epithelial cells. Basal laminae are deeper, on the tissue side of the epithelium.','Its interfacial properties change with compression and expansion. Its displayed width is enlarged to allow selection.','surf mech');
  window.lungDossiers=lung;
  window.alveoliDossiers=alveoli;
})();
